window.docContents[59].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.2.8. The Scalar Theory","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Classical-solutions":{"contents":"A finite-time pointwise solution consists of velocity and pressure on\n, the momentum equation, incompressibility, and the\nprescribed value at . The equation is imposed on the open positive-time\ndomain using totalized ambient derivatives, so a residual solution carries\nno differentiability.\n\nSmoothness at positive times turns the totalized derivatives into classical\nones.\n\nA finite-time smooth solution adds ambient smoothness at positive times and\none-sided continuity at the initial surface. A global pointwise residual\nsolution is posed on ; it restricts to every\nfinite positive horizon. Global smoothness is relative to the physical closed half-space\n, so nothing is assumed at negative times.\nRestricting a global smooth solution to a positive finite horizon gives a\nfinite-time smooth solution, smoothness included. As for Euler, a smooth\nsolution is a weak solution only once the  bounds, weak-gradient\nintegrability, decay, and spacetime integrability of the weak class are proved.\n\nWith classical derivatives available, the divergence of the momentum equation\ncan be taken as for Euler; the viscous term is the only new contribution.\n\nAt every positive-time point of a smooth Navier–Stokes solution,The viscosity does not alter this pressure source. For a componentwise\n incompressible velocity,The result is available for both finite-time and global smooth solutions.\n\nTake the spatial divergence of momentum. Local incompressibility makes the\ndivergence of  vanish. Commutation of the spatial divergence\nwith the vector Laplacian makes the viscous contribution vanish as well.\nThe remaining identity is exactly the pressure Poisson residual equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.3. Classical solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Classical-solutions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Traces-and-Integrated-Solutions":{"contents":"An element of  is an almost-everywhere equivalence class, so its\nvalue at a particular time has no meaning, and an equation in  almost\neverywhere does not by itself license writing u 0. Parabolic initial\nconditions and energy identities nevertheless require endpoint values.\nLions–Magenes regularity resolves this for a trajectory square integrable in\n whose weak time derivative is square integrable in . Time\nmollification proves the energy identity for smooth approximants, and the\nlimit selects a unique continuous -valued representative satisfying the\nenergy identity. This representative carries the initial trace and the\npointwise-in-time energy estimates.\n\nThe integrated formulation moves the time derivative from the trajectory onto\na scalar time test. It makes sense directly for a Bochner  class and\nrecords the initial datum through the boundary term created by integration by\nparts. It is well suited to weak convergence because it avoids selecting a\npointwise derivative for each approximation, and the Galerkin limit is taken\nin this formulation. When the form is strongly measurable and essentially\nbounded, a residual theorem recovers  as the weak \ntime derivative, Lions–Magenes supplies the continuous representative with\nits initial trace, and the energy identity gives uniqueness and continuous\ndependence under coercivity or a Gårding bound.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.3. Time Traces and Integrated Solutions","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Traces-and-Integrated-Solutions"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations","header":"9.3.1. The Direct Method","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Exact-Reductions":{"contents":"For a product , the heat equation becomes\n\n\n\nAfter division by  on the region where it is nonzero, the quotient\n depends only on  and the quotient  only on , so both\nequal a common separation constant. Boundary conditions then select the\nspatial eigenfunctions, and the time equation determines their decay. On an\ninterval, this produces sine modes and the familiar spectral heat series. We\nprove the product residual and the boundary-value modes, and convergence and\ntruncation estimates for an arbitrary Hilbert basis.\n\nTraveling and self-similar substitutions change the independent variables. For\n, the PDE reduces to an ODE for ; for\n, every derivative carries a power of\n determined by scaling. The chain-rule theorems are therefore the reusable\ncore. Transport, heat, Burgers, and singularity models use the same derivative\nidentities with different profile equations.\nThe KdV example constructs a localized  wave by\nbalancing nonlinear transport and dispersion. The porous-medium example\nconstructs the Barenblatt profile with any prescribed positive mass, proves\nthe weak PDE across its free boundary, and identifies its Dirac initial trace.\n\nCole–Hopf is more delicate because it divides by the transformed heat profile:\n\n\n\nThe nonvanishing domain belongs in the statement. On that domain, the heat\nresidual of  cancels the nonlinear Burgers residual of . For bounded\ncontinuous initial velocity and positive viscosity,\n constructs the heat profile itself:\nthe Gaussian integral of the exponential primitive is integrable and positive,\nand its logarithmic derivative attains the velocity in a joint space-time limit.\n\nA hodograph transform uses the unknown velocity as a new coordinate.\nA local Legendre transform does the same with the gradient of a scalar\nsolution. In both cases, an invertible Jacobian supplies the change of\ncoordinates. Following its inverse through the equation turns nonlinear\nplanar flow and minimal-surface equations into linear equations on the\nnew coordinate domain.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.1. Exact Reductions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Exact-Reductions"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--The-Completed-H___lder-Space":{"contents":"Let  be a metric type and let .\nHolderSpace.C0GammaOn Ω γ consists of:1. a bounded continuous function ; and2. evidence that  has a finite -Hölder constant.The first item supplies the uniform norm and global boundedness. The second\nsupplies quantitative regularity. Extensionality is pointwise: two elements\nare equal exactly when their values agree at every point of .There is no almost-everywhere quotient. This is the target in which Morrey\ntheory records the continuous representative selected from a\nSobolev equivalence class.\n\nThe structure certifies only that some Hölder constant is finite. Estimates\nneed a definite number attached to , and Mathlib's least admissible\nconstant supplies it without any new construction.\n\nFor , the bundled seminorm is exactlyThis least constant is nonnegative and satisfies the pointwise estimate:Thus later estimates can bound the seminorm once and immediately recover the\ncorresponding pointwise modulus of continuity.\n\nAdding the uniform norm to the seminorm turns it into a norm: the seminorm\nalone vanishes on constants, while the uniform norm alone forgets the modulus\nof continuity. Completeness of the sum norm is what makes the space a\nlegitimate target for bounded embedding operators.\n\nThe real vector space HolderSpace.C0GammaOn Ω γ carries the normBoth summands are bounded by the full norm, and this norm makes the space\ncomplete. Hence C0GammaOn Ω γ is a real Banach space for every metric type\n.\n\nThe triangle and scalar laws combine the corresponding laws for bounded\ncontinuous functions with Mathlib's subadditivity and homogeneity of\nnnHolderNorm.For completeness, let  be Cauchy in the displayed norm. The uniform\nparts are Cauchy in the Banach space of bounded continuous functions, so they\nconverge uniformly to a bounded continuous function . A tail of the\nsequence lies within distance one of a fixed term. Passing the pointwise\ndifference estimate to the uniform limit shows that this term minus  is\n-Hölder, hence so is the limit. Repeating this with smaller tolerances makes \nsmall in Hölder seminorm as well as uniformly, hence convergence in the\nfull norm.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Hölder Spaces","header":"4.3.3.2. The Completed Hölder Space","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--The-Completed-H___lder-Space"}});